The pingora server offers more detailed diagram for data flow.

https://github.com/cloudflare/pingora/blob/main/docs/user_guide/internals.md
```mermaid
 graph TD;
    start("new request")-->early_request_filter;
    early_request_filter-->request_filter;
    request_filter-->upstream_peer;

    upstream_peer-->Connect{{IO: connect to upstream}};

    Connect--connection success-->connected_to_upstream;
    Connect--connection failure-->fail_to_connect;

    connected_to_upstream-->upstream_request_filter;
    upstream_request_filter --> request_body_filter;
    request_body_filter --> SendReq{{IO: send request to upstream}};
    SendReq-->RecvResp{{IO: read response from upstream}};
    RecvResp-.feature: adjust_upstream_modules.->adjust_upstream_modules;
    adjust_upstream_modules-->upstream_response_filter-->response_filter-->upstream_response_body_filter-->response_body_filter-->logging-->endreq("request done");

    fail_to_connect --can retry-->upstream_peer;
    fail_to_connect --can't retry-->fail_to_proxy--send error response-->logging;

    RecvResp--failure-->IOFailure;
    SendReq--failure-->IOFailure;
    error_while_proxy--can retry-->upstream_peer;
    error_while_proxy--can't retry-->fail_to_proxy;

    request_filter --send response-->logging


    Error>any response filter error]-->error_while_proxy
    IOFailure>IO error]-->error_while_proxy
```
In order to create custom solution one has to implement trait.

Body inspection: https://dev.to/warren_jitsing_dd1c1d6fc6/pingora-guide-how-to-make-a-programmable-api-gateway-1oim#lesson-12-body-inspection

The sample with the link above works with streaming data, in order to work with whole body one can use the context structure.

In order to collect  body from streaming pingora api one needs to keep chunks in `buffer`
```
use bytes::BytesMut;

pub struct BodyCtx {
    pub buffer: BytesMut,
    pub is_sse: bool,
}

```
When chunk appears in http stream this trait function is called:
```
    type CTX = BodyCtx;
    async fn request_body_filter(
        &self,
        _session: &mut Session,
        body: &mut Option<Bytes>,
        end_of_stream: bool,
        ctx: &mut Self::CTX,
    ) -> Result<()> {
        if let Some(chunk) = body.take() {
            ctx.buffer.extend_from_slice(&chunk);
        }

        if end_of_stream {
            BodyInspector::check_body(&ctx.buffer)?;
            *body = Some(ctx.buffer.split().freeze());
        }

        Ok(())
    }
```
The `end_of_stream == true` means we collected all data and can process with `check_body` - parse, validate, update etc.
The response body works the same way, also this way we can validate, inspect, update sse chunks.

Sources: https://github.com/sco3/pin-body/tree/main
